Unarc (working name), test build
=================================

Unarc lists, extracts and makes archives on AROS. It comes as two programs
over one core: Unarc, a Zune program, and unarc-cli, a Shell command. A
third program, unarc-7z, reads 7z, RAR and ZIP with 7-Zip's own code; Unarc
and unarc-cli start it when they need it.

This is a test build for AROS ABIv11 on x86_64 (AROS One and the 2026.09
base). It does not run on mainline AROS (ABIv1).


Installing
----------

Copy the Unarc drawer anywhere, for example to SYS:Utilities or to a drawer
whose name has spaces. Nothing else is installed and nothing outside the
drawer is changed, apart from ENVARC:Unarc.prefs when you change a setting
in the start window. Keep unarc-7z in the same drawer as Unarc and
unarc-cli. To remove Unarc, delete the drawer (and ENVARC:Unarc.prefs).

No file type is associated with Unarc. To open archives with it from
Wanderer, give an archive a project icon whose default tool is Unarc, or set
Unarc as the default tool of the archive types in Wanderer's preferences.


The Zune program
----------------

Started on its own, Unarc opens a start window: drop archives on it to
extract them, or files and drawers to pack them. The buttons do the same
through file requesters.

Started with an archive (a double-click on an archive whose default tool is
Unarc), it extracts the archive next to itself into a new drawer and shows
what happened. The start window's "Double-clicked archives" setting, or the
tool type MODE=BROWSE, shows the contents first instead.

In the browser you can select entries and extract only those, extract everything,
or drag the selected entries onto an open Wanderer drawer window. Amiga+A
selects every entry. Several archives dropped at once are extracted one
after another; the progress window lists them and ends with one summary.

When a file already exists, Unarc asks. Cancel keeps the files already
finished and removes the one in progress. Nothing that was there before is
overwritten unless you say so.


The Shell command
-----------------

    unarc-cli <archive>                 extract next to the archive
    unarc-cli <archive> TO Work:Out     extract into Work:Out
    unarc-cli <archive> LIST            show the contents, write nothing
    unarc-cli new.zip PACK <files>      make an archive (.zip, .tar.gz, .7z)
    unarc-cli HELP                      all options, with examples
    unarc-cli FORMATS                   what each format supports

Return codes: 0 done, 5 done with notes (something skipped or renamed),
10 partly done, cancelled or stopped by an error, 20 nothing done. Ctrl-C
stops the command; finished files are kept.


Formats
-------

Extracting: ZIP (also ZIP64), 7z, RAR 1.5 to 5, LhA,
LZX, DMS, tar, tar.gz, tar.bz2, tar.xz, gzip, bzip2, xz, ISO 9660 and more.
Making: ZIP, tar.gz and 7z.

Multi-volume sets (name.part1.rar, name.rar + name.r00, name.7z.001) can be
opened through any of their volumes. A set with a volume missing is refused
before anything is written, and the message names the missing volume.

Archives with Amiga protection bits and comments (LhA, LZX) keep them.


Passwords
---------

Encrypted 7z, RAR and ZIP archives (ZipCrypto and AES) are read through
unarc-7z. When the list of entries itself is encrypted, Unarc asks for the
password at once; when only the data is, before anything is written. The
Shell command asks without showing what you type; in scripts, give it with
PASSWORD. Without a password, encrypted entries are left out and reported.
The password is not saved or logged anywhere.


unarc-7z
--------

unarc-7z is 7-Zip 26.04 (7z, RAR and ZIP handlers only), built for AROS as a
program of its own. Unarc uses it for:

- every RAR archive;
- encrypted 7z and ZIP archives;
- 7z and ZIP archives in a method the built-in reader lacks (for example
  Deflate64);
- 7z archives split into several files.

Plain 7z and ZIP archives are read by the built-in reader, which is faster.
Without unarc-7z, Unarc still reads 7z, RAR and ZIP as far as the built-in
reader can, without passwords, and says so when an archive needs unarc-7z.
An unarc-7z from another Unarc release is not used; install both from one
package.


Known limitations
-----------------

- Archive files over 2 GB: files over 2 GB inside an archive extract
  correctly, and a RAR set's volumes over 2 GB are found; but 7z and ZIP
  archives that are themselves over 2 GB have not been tested here.
- Names are shown and written in ISO-8859-1, AROS's character set. A name
  with characters outside it is changed, and Unarc lists such changes before
  extracting.
- Links stored in archives are not created; they are listed as not
  extracted.
- Disk images in archives (DMS) are listed but not written to a disk.


Licences
--------

Unarc's own code is under the MIT licence. It contains libarchive, XAD,
liblzma, zlib, bzip2 and GNU libiconv, each under its own licence; XAD and
GNU libiconv are under the GNU LGPL 2.1, which lets you rebuild Unarc with a
changed version of them. unarc-7z contains 7-Zip under the GNU LGPL 2.1, and
its RAR code comes with the unRAR licence restriction: it may not be used to
re-create the RAR compression algorithm. The full texts are in the Licences
drawer, and THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES there lists what is used where.

This test build comes from the aros-unarc source tree at the revision named
in the file Revision; the sources of the libraries are the upstream releases
named in THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.
